(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Static and Dynamic Hazards in combinational logic, and explain hazard-free circuit design.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Hazards and Glitches in Combinational Circuits:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition and Types of Hazards:) Tj
T*
(   - A hazard is an unwanted transient fluctuation \(glitch\) at the output of a combinational) Tj
T*
(   circuit caused by unequal propagation delays along converging signal paths.) Tj
T*
(   - Static-1 Hazard: Output is expected to remain stable at '1', but momentarily dips to '0') Tj
T*
(   during input transition.) Tj
T*
(   - Static-0 Hazard: Output is expected to remain stable at '0', but momentarily spikes to '1'.) Tj
T*
(   - Dynamic Hazard: Output changes three or more times during a single input transition \(e.g., 0) Tj
T*
(   -> 1 -> 0 -> 1\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Elimination of Static-1 Hazards:) Tj
T*
(   - In a K-map, a static-1 hazard occurs whenever an input transition moves between two adjacent) Tj
T*
(   minterm groupings that do NOT overlap.) Tj
T*
(   - Solution: Add redundant consensus prime implicants that physically bridge the two adjacent) Tj
T*
(   groupings.) Tj
T*
(   - Example: F = A'C + AB. Transition from ABC = 111 to 011 switches from AB to A'C. If path) Tj
T*
(   through inverter A' is delayed, both terms drop to 0 momentarily.) Tj
T*
(   - Adding consensus term BC gives: F = A'C + AB + BC. During transition, BC remains stable 1,) Tj
T*
(   eliminating the glitch.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
