(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the High-Frequency Hybrid-Pi Model of a BJT and derive the expression for Miller capacitance.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   High-Frequency Hybrid-Pi Model and Miller Effect:) Tj
T*
(   1. Small-Signal High-Frequency Equivalent Circuit:) Tj
T*
(   - Includes intrinsic base-spreading resistance rbb', base-emitter dynamic resistance r_b'e \(or) Tj
T*
(   r_pi\), transconductance gm = Ic / Vt.) Tj
T*
(   - Diffusion capacitance C_pi \(or C_b'e\) across EBJ: accounts for base minority carrier charge) Tj
T*
(   storage.) Tj
T*
(   - Depletion capacitance C_mu \(or C_b'c\) across reverse-biased CBJ.) Tj
T*
(   2. Miller's Theorem Derivation:) Tj
T*
(   - Feedback capacitance C_mu is connected between input node B' and output node C.) Tj
T*
(   - Voltage gain of the stage: Av = Vc / Vb' approx - gm * R_L'.) Tj
T*
(   - Current through C_mu: I_mu = \(Vb' - Vc\) * s * C_mu = Vb' * [ 1 - \(-Av\) ] * s * C_mu = Vb' * [) Tj
T*
(   1 + Av ] * s * C_mu.) Tj
T*
(   - Equivalent input admittance: Yin = I_mu / Vb' = s * [ C_mu * \(1 + Av\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Equivalent Input Miller Capacitance:) Tj
T*
(   C_in_Miller = C_mu * \(1 + |Av|\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Impact on Bandwidth:) Tj
T*
(   - Total input capacitance: C_in = C_pi + C_mu * \(1 + |Av|\).) Tj
T*
(   - Because voltage gain |Av| is large \(e.g., 200\), even a tiny C_mu \(e.g., 2 pF\) appears across) Tj
T*
(   the input as 400 pF!) Tj
T*
(   - This huge Miller capacitance severely restricts the high-frequency 3-dB cut-off bandwidth of) Tj
T*
(   the amplifier.) Tj
T*
