/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Programmable Logic Devices \(PLDs\): Contrast PROM, PAL, and PLA architectures.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Architectural Comparison of PLDs \(PROM, PAL, PLA\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Programmable Read-Only Memory \(PROM\):) Tj
T*
(   - Architecture: Fixed AND array \(full-input decoder\) + Programmable OR array.) Tj
T*
(   - All 2^n minterms are decoded permanently. The user programs connections in the OR array.) Tj
T*
(   - Advantage: Simple programming; can generate any Boolean function.) Tj
T*
(   - Drawback: Impractical for functions with large number of input variables \(decoder size) Tj
T*
(   explodes exponentially: 2^n\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Programmable Array Logic \(PAL\):) Tj
T*
(   - Architecture: Programmable AND array + Fixed OR array.) Tj
T*
(   - Product terms can be customized; OR gates have fixed inputs.) Tj
T*
(   - Advantage: Faster propagation speeds than PLA; low silicon area.) Tj
T*
(   - Drawback: Limited flexibility if a function requires more product terms than the fixed OR) Tj
T*
(   gate inputs.) Tj
T*
(   3. Programmable Logic Array \(PLA\):) Tj
T*
(   - Architecture: Programmable AND array + Programmable OR array.) Tj
T*
(   - Maximum architectural flexibility: product terms can be shared across multiple output) Tj
T*
(   functions.) Tj
T*
(   - Complex programming and slightly longer propagation delay due to dual programmable arrays.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
