BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 25:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the C-Filter, L-Filter, and Pi-Section Filter for full-wave rectifiers, deriving ripple factor equations.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Smoothing Filters for Rectifier Circuits:) Tj
T*
(   1. Shunt Capacitor Filter \(C-Filter\):) Tj
T*
(   - Capacitor C connected in parallel with load RL charges to peak voltage Vm during conduction) Tj
T*
(   and discharges exponentially through RL during non-conduction.) Tj
T*
(   - Ripple Factor Derivation:) Tj
T*
(   Approximating discharge as linear: Delta-V = V_ripple_pp = Vm / \(2 * f * C * RL\).) Tj
T*
(   RMS ripple voltage: V_ac = V_ripple_pp / \(2 * sqrt\(3\)\).) Tj
T*
(   Ripple Factor: r = V_ac / V_dc = 1 / [ 4 * sqrt\(3\) * f * C * RL ].) Tj
T*
(   - Ripple increases with increasing load current \(decreasing RL\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Series Inductor Filter \(L-Filter\):) Tj
T*
(   - Choke L connected in series with load opposes changes in current by Lenz's law.) Tj
T*
(   - Ripple Factor: r = R_L / [ 3 * sqrt\(2\) * omega * L ].) Tj
T*
(   - Ripple DECREASES with increasing load current \(superior for heavy industrial loads\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Pi-Section \(C-L-C\) Filter:) Tj
T*
(   - Combines shunt capacitor C1, series inductor L, and second shunt capacitor C2.) Tj
T*
