(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Design of a Look-Up Table \(LUT\) in Field Programmable Gate Arrays \(FPGAs\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   FPGA Look-Up Tables \(LUT\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Concept:) Tj
T*
(   - Rather than assembling discrete hardwired logic gates, modern FPGAs \(Xilinx, Altera\)) Tj
T*
(   implement combinational logic using small static RAM look-up tables \(LUTs\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Architecture of a K-input LUT:) Tj
T*
(   - Consists of 2^K SRAM memory cells and a 2^K-to-1 multiplexer driven by K input address lines.) Tj
T*
(   - The truth table of any arbitrary K-variable function is pre-loaded into the SRAM bits during) Tj
T*
(   FPGA configuration.) Tj
T*
(   - When inputs are applied to the select lines, the MUX routes the corresponding stored output) Tj
T*
(   bit to the output.) Tj
T*
(   3. Flexibility:) Tj
T*
(   - A single 6-input LUT \(LUT6\) can implement ANY of the 2^\(2^6\) = 2^64 possible 6-variable logic) Tj
T*
(   functions in exactly the same uniform propagation delay \(~0.2 ns\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
